course: dictionary
lesson: 9173
title: Snuffle: noisy breathing and sniffing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound like what they mean. Say snuffle out loud and you can almost hear it: a short, damp sniffing sound through the nose. Good readers listen to a word's sound as well as its meaning.
explain: «Hedgehogs snore, snuffle and grunt.» Snore, snuffle and grunt are all sounds. Snore is a noisy breath in sleep and grunt is a low noise, so snuffle must be a noise too, the sniffing sound.
explain: Now read this: «At night it snuffles and grunts through gardens hunting beetles, worms, caterpillars and slugs.» The clues are night, gardens and hunting. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Sniffing along the ground for food fits best, so that is what snuffling means.
explain: Snuffle can also be a noun. «The snuffles» is an informal name for a light cold with a runny nose, the kind that makes your breathing loud. If someone has the snuffles, they are poorly, not hunting for food.
fact: Snuffle is a verb, so its forms are snuffles, snuffled and snuffling.
fact: To snuffle means to breathe noisily through your nose, for example when you have a cold or are crying.
fact: When an animal snuffles, it sniffs noisily along the ground, looking for food or a smell.
fact: «The badger snuffling at dusk» shows a badger sniffing for food as the day ends.
fact: A snuffle is the noisy breathing sound itself, and «the snuffles» is an informal name for a light cold.
exercise: «At night it snuffles and grunts through gardens hunting beetles, worms, caterpillars and slugs.» What is the hedgehog doing when it snuffles? | Flying over the garden | Sleeping in a nest of leaves | Singing to its babies | *Sniffing along the ground for food
exercise: Ella's little brother is crying, and his breath is loud through his nose. Which word describes this? | Galloping | *Snuffling | Whistling | Hibernating
exercise: Dad says he has the snuffles. What is wrong with him? | *He has a light cold with a runny nose | He is hunting for worms in the garden | He has broken his leg | He is hibernating in a nest of leaves
test: «The badger snuffling at dusk» means the badger is... | Singing loudly in the trees | Asleep in a warm nest | *Sniffing noisily for food as it gets dark | Running away from the children
test: Which word is the past tense of snuffle? | snuffle | snuffles | snuffling | *snuffled
exam: Which sentence uses snuffle correctly? | The hedgehog snuffled a happy song to the moon | The hedgehog snuffled the bright, sparkling sea | *The hedgehog snuffled through the leaves, looking for slugs | The hedgehog snuffled over the roof and into the sky
